# deploy/microvm/microvm-worker.service
#
# Runs cmd/microvm-worker (see remote-worker/cmd/microvm-worker/main.go), the process
# that creates every VM's cgroup under microvm-vms.slice for the lifetime of the
# process (Task 17, spec §6's #1 practical failure: "Worker crash leaks VMs").
#
# WHERE EACH CGROUP ACTUALLY LANDS, because the final whole-branch review's H1 was a
# false premise about exactly this. Slice=microvm-vms.slice below makes systemd nest
# THIS UNIT's cgroup under that slice (systemd.slice(5): a unit assigned to a slice is
# placed beneath it in the cgroup tree -- the same shape as system.slice/sshd.service),
# while both launcher arms place each VM's cgroup under the same slice as a SIBLING of
# this unit, not as a child of it:
#
#   /sys/fs/cgroup/microvm-vms.slice/
#   |-- microvm-worker.service/   <-- this unit; cgroup.procs holds the worker's own pid
#   |-- vm-9/                     <-- Firecracker: jailer --parent-cgroup + --cgroup
#   `-- vm-vm-9.scope/            <-- Cloud Hypervisor: systemd-run --scope --slice=
#
# Two consequences follow, and both are load-bearing:
#
#  1. KillMode=control-group below kills what is in THIS UNIT's control group, which is
#     the worker (and any process it left in its own cgroup) -- NOT the sibling VM
#     cgroups. Tearing those down on a clean stop is `systemctl stop
#     microvm-vms.slice`, which stops the whole slice subtree. An earlier version of
#     this comment claimed KillMode=control-group reached the VM cgroups; it does not,
#     and stating it did is what made the crash-recovery sweep look optional.
#  2. Because the worker is a sibling of the VM cgroups rather than their parent,
#     SweepOrphans (called from main(), before Probe) must be able to tell them apart.
#     It does: it sweeps only directory names this pool itself created and never its
#     own cgroup (see cgroup.go's H1 block). It is the crash-recovery half of the
#     mitigation regardless -- a kill-9/OOM/power-loss leaves VM cgroups behind no
#     matter which cgroup the worker occupied.
#
# Slice=microvm-vms.slice is KEPT deliberately, not left in place by inertia: it puts
# the worker's own memory in the same accounting scope as the VMs it admits (which is
# what SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB is reasoning about), and it makes the slice one unit an
# operator can stop or inspect to reach the whole tier. Moving the worker out would
# make "every child of the slice is a VM" true, but it would NOT remove the need for
# the sweep, and it would split the tier across two accounting scopes to buy a
# simplification the sweep no longer needs.

[Unit]
Description=microvm-worker: ephemeral microVM sandbox tier (moca P4)
Documentation=https://github.com/rossoctl/moca
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# Spec §6: "KVM unavailable at startup ... Fail the unit at start with an explicit
# message; never fall back to running commands on the host." AssertPathExists fails
# the unit before ExecStart even runs, which is a stronger, earlier form of that
# posture than leaving it to the process's own vmpool.New/Probe checks alone.
AssertPathExists=/dev/kvm
# A permanently-broken host must reach `failed`, not restart forever.
#
# Restart=on-failure with RestartSec=5s and systemd's DEFAULTS
# (DefaultStartLimitBurst=5 over DefaultStartLimitIntervalSec=10s) never trips the rate
# limiter: at one start per 5s at most 2-3 starts land in any 10s window, so the unit
# sits in activating/auto-restart indefinitely while `systemctl is-failed` reports
# success and monitoring sees a healthy host. This branch has already produced two
# crash-loops of exactly that shape (an unstated jailer path, a missing snapshot), and
# every remaining startup-failure path still reaches it: no /srv/snapshots/default, a
# manifest hash mismatch, the SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB refusal, a VMM socket already in use.
#
# 5 starts per 5 minutes: a transient failure (a slow /dev/kvm, a race with a mount)
# still gets its retries, and a host that is simply broken lands in `failed` inside half
# a minute and pages someone. Both keys belong in [Unit], not [Service] -- systemd 255
# reports "Unknown key name 'StartLimitIntervalSec' in section 'Service'" and ignores
# them there, which is the silent no-op this comment exists to prevent someone
# reintroducing.
StartLimitIntervalSec=300
StartLimitBurst=5
# The admission budget below (SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB) is a promise about how much memory
# this host has, and nothing in the worker reads MemTotal. On a host with less RAM than
# the budget the gate therefore cannot refuse before the OOM killer arrives -- it admits
# VMs against memory that does not exist, and spec §7.4's prediction 1 ("replenishment
# binds on process/memory count before CPU") gets "confirmed" by a gate that never
# refuses at all. Every other invariant in this tier fails at start rather than trusting
# the operator (AssertPathExists=/dev/kvm above, SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB being mandatory,
# Config.Normalize's cross-field checks); the one that was missing is the check against
# the host this unit is actually running on. A comment is not a control.
#
# AssertMemory compares the amount of PHYSICAL memory in the system (in a container, the
# memory assigned to it) -- systemd's ConditionMemory family, available since v244 -- so
# it is the same fact the budget is asserting, read from the host. Assertion rather than
# Condition deliberately: a Condition silently skips the start, an Assert fails it
# loudly. And like AssertPathExists it does NOT trigger Restart=, so it cannot produce
# the crash-loop the StartLimit keys above exist to bound.
#
# 23G against a 24 GiB SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB, not 24G: physical memory here is MemTotal,
# which is always somewhat BELOW nominal RAM (firmware and kernel reservations), so a
# threshold equal to the budget would refuse to start on a genuine 24 GiB host. The band
# is deliberate -- this catches "the budget is bigger than the machine" (a 16 GiB host
# refuses), not a 1% discrepancy. deploy/microvm/tests/systemd-units.test.sh does that
# arithmetic against SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB below and against that tolerance, so raising
# either value alone is a test failure rather than a discovery made by the OOM killer.
AssertMemory=>=23G

[Service]
Type=simple
Slice=microvm-vms.slice
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/microvm-worker
KillMode=control-group
KillSignal=SIGTERM
TimeoutStopSec=30
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s

# RLIMIT_MEMLOCK: PinMemoryFile (memfile mlock, spec §4.3) and, per VM, the guest's
# own memory need to be lockable. main() also calls vmpool.RaiseMemlockLimit() at
# startup to raise the soft limit to the hard limit within whatever this unit grants,
# but the unit's own ceiling is the hard cap that call cannot exceed -- infinity here
# is deliberate for exactly the reason spec §7.5 names: a kernel limit mistaken for a
# density ceiling fails at 500 VMs after working at 20, indistinguishably from the
# real thing, so this must not be the thing that silently caps density.
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity
# Every VM's vsock/API socket, plus the relay connection and whatever standby depth
# is configured, each cost file descriptors; spec §7.5 names nofile as one of the
# kernel limits that must be raised deliberately rather than discovered at 500 VMs.
LimitNOFILE=1048576

# The VMM binaries this tier cannot run without, stated explicitly rather than left to
# the code defaults (/usr/bin/jailer, /usr/bin/firecracker). Verified on hardware: with
# these unset the worker starts, sweeps correctly, and then dies in its startup probe with
# "start jailer: fork/exec /usr/bin/jailer: no such file or directory", crash-looping every
# 5s. Firecracker's own release tarball and this unit's own ExecStart both live under
# /usr/local/bin, so /usr/bin was an unstated assumption the unit disagreed with itself
# about. Override per-host if your packaging installs them elsewhere.
Environment=SH_JAILER_BIN=/usr/local/bin/jailer
Environment=SH_FIRECRACKER_BIN=/usr/local/bin/firecracker
# The golden snapshot is SH_SNAPSHOT_DIR/SH_SNAPSHOT_IMAGE (main.go joins the two), so the
# deployed snapshot lives at /srv/snapshots/default unless this is changed. Stated rather
# than relying on the code default, because a missing image directory presents as a
# device-check failure naming a path nobody configured.
Environment=SH_SNAPSHOT_IMAGE=default
Environment=SH_VMM=firecracker
Environment=SH_SNAPSHOT_DIR=/srv/snapshots
Environment=SH_WORKSPACE_ROOT=/srv/workspaces
Environment=SH_CHROOT_BASE=/srv/jail
# Slice-RELATIVE, and spelling the full systemd hierarchy. Both halves were wrong
# before, each in a way that looked right. Verified on hardware (jailer v1.17.0,
# cgroup v2): jailer REFUSES an absolute --parent-cgroup ("Path should not be
# absolute"), so every restore failed; and a bare "microvm-vms.slice" created a new
# top-level cgroup OUTSIDE this slice, with memory.max set on it so it looked correct
# while the slice accounted none of it. systemd expands a dashed slice name into a
# hierarchy, so this unit's own Slice=microvm-vms.slice lives at
# /sys/fs/cgroup/microvm.slice/microvm-vms.slice -- and /sys/fs/cgroup/microvm-vms.slice
# does not exist at all, which is why the orphan sweep silently swept nothing.
Environment=SH_PARENT_CGROUP=microvm.slice/microvm-vms.slice
Environment=SH_STANDBY_DEPTH=2
Environment=SH_GUEST_RAM_MB=256
Environment=SH_MAX_RUNS=64
Environment=SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB=24576
Environment=SH_MEMORY_RESERVE_MB=4096

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
